1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,433
Hi, I'm Chandra, a first-year student in the Master of Artificial Intelligence Systems

2
00:00:05,433 --> 00:00:08,529
Management program at Carnegie Mellon University.

3
00:00:08,529 --> 00:00:13,773
This video is my submission to the AI Horizons Pittsburgh hackathon, housing track.

4
00:00:13,773 --> 00:00:19,270
I built an AI-powered dashboard for exploring property sale prices in Allegheny County.

5
00:00:18,578 --> 00:00:24,146
The county publishes every property sale, but the raw data is messy.

6
00:00:24,146 --> 00:00:29,715
Out of three hundred sixty-seven thousand records from 2016 to 2026,

7
00:00:29,715 --> 00:00:35,365
almost a third are one-dollar deeds, family transfers, or duplicates.

8
00:00:35,365 --> 00:00:40,688
So I built a cleaning pipeline, an interactive map and dashboard,

9
00:00:40,688 --> 00:00:47,976
and a question-and-answer assistant powered by Claude, that is honest about data quality.

10
00:00:48,279 --> 00:00:51,237
Here is the dashboard, running live.

11
00:00:51,237 --> 00:00:55,018
The filter bar at the top controls everything:

12
00:00:55,018 --> 00:00:58,551
which sales to include, and the year range.

13
00:00:58,551 --> 00:01:05,290
By default, it uses only sales the county assessor validated as real market sales.

14
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:09,892
The 2025 median is two hundred seventy thousand dollars,

15
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:12,768
up seventy-four percent since 2016.

16
00:01:13,074 --> 00:01:19,839
The map shows every municipality, with Pittsburgh split into its thirty-two wards.

17
00:01:19,839 --> 00:01:24,789
I built these shapes from the county's parcel boundary file,

18
00:01:24,789 --> 00:01:28,172
merging more than half a million parcels.

19
00:01:28,172 --> 00:01:33,534
Hovering shows the median price and how many sales are behind it.

20
00:01:33,534 --> 00:01:39,969
Grey places have fewer than thirty sales, so a noisy number never looks solid.

21
00:01:40,276 --> 00:01:46,859
Clicking a place zooms in and puts every individual sale on the map.

22
00:01:46,859 --> 00:01:48,989
Here is Mount Lebanon.

23
00:01:48,989 --> 00:01:57,121
The card shows the median, the price change, and the sample size behind each number.

24
00:01:57,121 --> 00:02:03,220
Clicking a dot shows the address, price, and date of that sale.

25
00:02:03,528 --> 00:02:07,051
Now I switch the map to price change.

26
00:02:07,051 --> 00:02:10,193
About half the county turns grey,

27
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:16,191
because the assessor validated very few sales in 2016 and 2017.

28
00:02:16,191 --> 00:02:23,712
That is a real data quality problem, and the map shows it instead of hiding it.

29
00:02:23,712 --> 00:02:28,949
Switching to all priced sales fills most of it back in.

30
00:02:28,949 --> 00:02:36,566
And with the Since 2020 preset, every panel updates to the post-pandemic market.

31
00:02:36,882 --> 00:02:44,295
Below the map, the trend chart compares the places I clicked with the whole county,

32
00:02:44,295 --> 00:02:49,923
and the municipality table ranks all one hundred thirty places,

33
00:02:49,923 --> 00:02:54,031
with low sample badges where the data is thin.

34
00:02:55,124 --> 00:03:01,240
The data quality panel documents every cleaning step, how many rows each one removed,

35
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:04,407
and how validation coverage changes by year.

36
00:03:04,714 --> 00:03:06,010
Now the AI part.

37
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:10,224
From the map card, I ask Claude about Mount Lebanon.

38
00:03:10,224 --> 00:03:13,465
Claude never sees the raw data directly.

39
00:03:13,465 --> 00:03:17,841
It calls read-only query tools on the cleaned dataset,

40
00:03:17,841 --> 00:03:21,325
and the interface lists every query it ran.

41
00:03:21,636 --> 00:03:24,545
The answer streams in with a table.

42
00:03:24,545 --> 00:03:31,444
It gives the sample size behind each number, cross-checks against all priced sales,

43
00:03:31,444 --> 00:03:33,522
and ends with data notes.

44
00:03:33,522 --> 00:03:37,845
Every number traces back to a query you can inspect.

45
00:03:38,159 --> 00:03:41,123
Follow-up questions keep the context.

46
00:03:41,123 --> 00:03:45,690
When I ask about Haysville, one of the smallest boroughs,

47
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:51,057
Claude says plainly that a handful of sales cannot support a trend,

48
00:03:51,057 --> 00:03:53,060
instead of inventing one.

49
00:04:01,996 --> 00:04:04,689
To be clear about what is real:

50
00:04:04,689 --> 00:04:07,294
everything you saw is working.

51
00:04:07,294 --> 00:04:14,591
The data, the map, the charts, and Claude's answers are live, and nothing is mocked.

52
00:04:14,591 --> 00:04:15,981
The limitations:

53
00:04:15,981 --> 00:04:19,455
it runs locally and is not deployed yet.

54
00:04:19,455 --> 00:04:26,751
The county sales file has no property type, so medians mix houses, condos, and land.

55
00:04:26,751 --> 00:04:28,749
2026 is a partial year.

56
00:04:28,749 --> 00:04:35,090
And in testing, Claude once drew a wrong conclusion from correct numbers,

57
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:38,825
so answers should still be read critically.

58
00:04:38,825 --> 00:04:43,428
I built this with Claude Code as my coding assistant.

59
00:04:43,735 --> 00:04:49,638
Next steps are deploying it, adding property types from the county assessment data,

60
00:04:49,638 --> 00:04:54,189
and letting people compare their own home with its neighborhood.

61
00:04:54,189 --> 00:04:55,825
Thank you for watching.
